# The guide "How MX works". Read by compose.py after aps_guide.py and xrf_guide.py (it uses _panel, _sl and _slw from them).
# Its figures are in mx.js.

# ---------- equation cards ----------
EQ['m_res']=card('How fine a detail light can show',r"d_{\min}\approx\frac{\lambda}{2}\qquad\qquad \lambda\,[\text{Å}]=\frac{12.398}{E\,[\text{keV}]}",
 [(r"d_{\min}","The smallest spacing between two things that can still be told apart."),(r"\lambda","The wavelength of the light."),("E","The energy of one photon of that light.")],
 "A wave cannot report on detail much finer than half its own length. Shorter waves show finer detail, and shorter waves are photons of higher energy. This is why a light microscope stops at about a quarter of a micron, and why seeing atoms takes X-rays.",
 "Green light, 500 nm: about 250 nm, which is 1,700 times the distance between two bonded atoms. X-rays of 12.662 keV: a wavelength of 0.979 Å, short enough in principle to show detail of half an ångström. In practice the crystal sets the limit, not the wavelength.")
EQ['m_lat']=card('Many copies scattering in step',r"I_N(q)=\left|F(q)\right|^{2}\;\frac{\sin^{2}(\pi N q a)}{\sin^{2}(\pi q a)}",
 [("I_N","The brightness scattered by a row of N identical molecules."),("F(q)","The wave scattered by one molecule in the direction q. Its square is the dashed red line in the figure."),("q","A measure of the scattering angle: one over the size of detail that direction reports on."),("a","The distance from one molecule to the next.")],
 "The fraction is close to zero in most directions and rises to N² wherever q times a is a whole number. So the scatter of N copies is gathered into spots a distance 1/a apart, each N² times as bright as one molecule and 1/N as wide. The energy in each spot grows in step with the number of copies. Where the spots are depends only on the lattice. How strong each one is depends only on the molecule.",
 "A crystal 50 µm across with a repeat of 100 Å has 5,000 unit cells along each edge, and about a hundred billion in all. Every spot holds a hundred billion times the energy that one unit cell would send in that direction.")
EQ['m_bragg']=card("Bragg's law: when a spot appears",r"2\,d\,\sin\theta=n\,\lambda",
 [("d","The spacing of one set of parallel planes through the crystal lattice."),(r"\theta","The angle between the beam and those planes. The beam is turned through twice this."),(r"\lambda","The X-ray wavelength."),("n","A whole number.")],
 "Each spot can be thought of as a reflection of the beam from one set of planes in the crystal. The reflections from successive planes add up only when the extra path from one plane to the next is a whole number of wavelengths. For a given set of planes that happens at one angle only, which is why the crystal has to be turned. Fine spacings reflect at large angles, so the fine detail of the structure is at the edge of the detector.",
 "At 12.662 keV, 0.979 Å: planes 50 Å apart turn the beam through 1.1°, planes 2 Å apart through 28°, and planes 1 Å apart through 59°.")
EQ['m_spacing']=card('How far apart the spots are',r"\Delta\approx\frac{\lambda\,D}{a}",
 [(r"\Delta","The distance between neighboring spots on the detector."),("D","The distance from the crystal to the detector."),("a","The length of the unit cell, the repeating unit of the crystal.")],
 "The pattern is the reverse of the crystal: the larger the repeating unit, the closer the spots. A crystal with a very large unit cell needs the detector moved back, or a more parallel beam, to keep its spots from running into each other.",
 "At 0.979 Å with the detector 300 mm away: a 100 Å cell puts the spots 2.9 mm apart, and a 400 Å cell 0.7 mm apart.")
EQ['m_sf']=card('What sets the strength and phase of a spot',r"F(hkl)=\sum_{j}f_j\;e^{\,2\pi i\,(h x_j+k y_j+l z_j)}",
 [("F(hkl)","The structure factor of the spot with indices h, k and l: a wave with a strength and a phase."),("f_j","How strongly atom j scatters: roughly its number of electrons, falling off at larger angles."),("x_j,\\ y_j,\\ z_j","The position of atom j, as fractions of the unit cell.")],
 "Every atom in the unit cell contributes to every spot. Each contribution is a small wave whose phase depends on where the atom sits. Where the contributions agree the spot is strong, and where they cancel it is weak. So no single spot belongs to any one atom: the whole structure is spread over the whole pattern.",
 None)
EQ['m_synth']=card('From the spots back to the density',r"\rho(x,y,z)=\frac{1}{V}\sum_{hkl}\left|F(hkl)\right|\,\cos\!\bigl[2\pi(hx+ky+lz)-\varphi(hkl)\bigr]",
 [(r"\rho","The electron density at the point x, y, z of the unit cell."),("V","The volume of the unit cell."),(r"\left|F\right|","The strength of each spot, which is measured."),(r"\varphi","The phase of each spot, which is not.")],
 "The density is a sum of waves, one for each spot. The spot's indices give the wave's direction and spacing, its strength gives the wave's height, and its phase says where the crests lie. This sum is the job a lens does in a microscope. It needs both numbers for every spot.",
 None)
EQ['m_int']=card('What the detector keeps',r"I(hkl)\propto\left|F(hkl)\right|^{2}",
 [("I(hkl)","The brightness of a spot, which is what the detector records.")],
 "The detector counts photons. That gives the square of the strength of each wave and nothing about its phase. This is the phase problem. It is not a flaw of the detector: X-rays vibrate about three billion billion times a second, and nothing can follow that.",
 None)
EQ['m_obs']=card('Measurements against unknowns',r"N_{\text{spots}}\approx\frac{2\pi}{3}\,\frac{V}{d^{3}}\qquad\qquad N_{\text{unknowns}}\approx 4\,N_{\text{atoms}}",
 [(r"N_{\text{spots}}","The number of different spots out to resolution d, counting a spot and its opposite on the far side of the pattern as one. Symmetry in the crystal lowers it further."),("V","The volume of the unit cell."),(r"N_{\text{unknowns}}","Three coordinates and one B-factor for each atom. The B-factor says how much the atom moves or varies from cell to cell.")],
 "The number of spots rises steeply as the resolution improves: twice the resolution, eight times the spots. The number of unknowns stays the same. At high resolution the data outnumber the unknowns many times. At low resolution they do not, and the model leans on what is already known of bond lengths and angles.",
 "A unit cell 100 Å on each side, half protein and half solvent, holds about 28,500 atoms of protein, not counting hydrogen or the solvent: 114,000 unknowns. At 2 Å it gives about 260,000 spots, a little over two for each unknown. At 3 Å it gives 78,000, fewer than the unknowns.")
EQ['m_R']=card('The R-factor',r"R=\frac{\sum\bigl|\,|F_{\text{obs}}|-|F_{\text{calc}}|\,\bigr|}{\sum|F_{\text{obs}}|}",
 [(r"F_{\text{obs}}","The strength of each spot as measured."),(r"F_{\text{calc}}","The strength of the same spot calculated from the model."),("R","The overall disagreement. R-free is the same sum over a small set of spots kept out of the refinement.")],
 "R is the total disagreement between the measured spots and the model, as a fraction of the total. Refinement lowers it. R alone can be driven down by adding detail the data do not support, so a few percent of the spots are set aside from the start and never used. Their R, called R-free, falls only if the model is truly getting better.",
 "Atoms placed at random give an R of about 59%. A finished structure at 2 Å typically has R near 20% and R-free a few points higher. A wide gap between the two is a warning.")
EQ['m_dose']=card('How the spots fade with dose',r"I(D)\approx I_0\;2^{-D/D_{1/2}}\qquad\qquad D_{1/2}\approx 10\ \text{MGy}\times d\,[\text{Å}]",
 [("D","The dose: the energy the crystal has absorbed from the beam for each kilogram of its mass. 1 Gy is 1 joule per kilogram."),("I_0,\\ I","The brightness of a spot at the start and after the dose D."),("D_{1/2}","The dose that halves the brightness of spots carrying detail of size d.")],
 "A rule of thumb for crystals held at 100 K. The damage disorders the crystal a little at a time, and fine detail is the first to go. A commonly used limit for a whole data set is 30 MGy. The dose a crystal can take depends very little on how fast it is delivered, so a brighter beam means shorter exposures, not more data from each crystal. More data needs more crystal: a larger one, or many of them.",
 "After 20 MGy the 2 Å spots are at half their brightness and the 4 Å spots at 71%. After 30 MGy: 35% and 59%.")

# ---------- figure panels ----------
FIG['m_hero']=_panel('mxHeroCv','Animation in three parts: a crystal turns in the X-ray beam; the detector takes image after image, with the spots falling in curved bands that move as the crystal turns; and from the spots comes a map, into which atoms are fitted, until the view pulls back to show the whole protein, myoglobin',None,None,
 "The whole method in one line. A crystal turns in the X-ray beam, and the detector takes thousands of images of the spots it throws. From the spots a map of the electrons is worked out, atoms are fitted into the map, and the result is a model of the molecule, atom by atom. The protein here is myoglobin, drawn from its real structure. The images are a simulation of a small made-up crystal on the detector of 24-ID-C, and the map shows one helix of the protein.")
_GS=[('mb','Myoglobin'),('hb','Hemoglobin'),('ncp','A nucleosome'),('igg','An antibody'),('gro','GroEL and GroES'),('ribo','A ribosome'),('vir','The shell of a cold virus')]
FIG['m_gal']=_panel('galCv','Interactive: seven structures drawn to one scale, from myoglobin through hemoglobin, a nucleosome, an antibody, GroEL with GroES and a ribosome to the shell of a rhinovirus, with a scale bar of 100 angstroms',
 ['<div class="ctl"><label for="gSel">Structure</label><select id="gSel">'+_opts(_GS,'ribo')+'</select></div>'],
 [('Mass','gM'),('Atoms, not counting hydrogen','gA'),('Across','gS'),('What it is','gW')],
 "Seven structures that crystallography has solved, drawn to the same scale. Choose one from the menu, or click on it. The smallest, myoglobin, has about 1,260 atoms. The largest here has several hundred thousand, and crystallography placed nearly every one. Each colored shape is one chain, or one folded part of a chain. In the nucleosome and the ribosome the orange and yellow are DNA and RNA. Myoglobin is drawn from its real chain, entry 1MBN in the Protein Data Bank, with its helices in blue. The others are cartoons drawn from the known sizes and arrangements of the molecules, not from their deposited coordinates. Masses, sizes and atom counts are rounded.")
FIG['m_scale']=_panel('scaleCv','Interactive: a ruler from one angstrom to a tenth of a millimeter with a bond between atoms, DNA, hemoglobin, a ribosome, a virus, a bacterium, a red blood cell and a protein crystal on it, and a marker for the finest detail that light of the chosen wavelength can show',
 ['<div class="btns" style="grid-column:1/-1"><button id="zBV">Visible light</button><button id="zBX">X-rays at NE-CAT</button></div>',_slw('zL','Wavelength of the light','500 nm',-10.5,-6,0.01,-6.3)],
 [('Kind of light','zK'),('Energy of one photon','zE'),('Finest detail it can show','zD')],
 "A ruler from one ångström to a tenth of a millimeter. Each step to the right is ten times larger. The things in the shaded part are big enough for the chosen light to show, and the rest are faded. With visible light a bacterium can be seen and a virus cannot. To see the atoms of a protein the wavelength has to come down to about an ångström, and light of that wavelength is an X-ray. Half a wavelength is the usual rough limit. Sizes are typical values.")
FIG['m_lat']=_panel('latCv','Interactive: a row of identical molecules, and below it the brightness they scatter against angle, which gathers into sharp spots as more copies are added',
 [_sl('lN','Copies of the molecule','3',1,40,1,3),_sl('lA','Distance from one copy to the next','14 Å',8,40,1,14)],
 [('Brightest spot, against one molecule','lB'),('Width of each spot','lW'),('Number of spots','lS')],
 "Top: a row of identical molecules, each drawn as four atoms. Bottom: how brightly the row scatters in each direction. The dashed red line is the pattern of one molecule alone, which is weak and smooth. Add copies and the scatter gathers into sharp spots, because only in certain directions do the waves from every copy arrive in step. Each copy added makes the spots brighter and narrower. Their heights follow the red line: the molecule decides how strong each spot is, and the lattice decides where the spots are. Move the copies further apart and the spots crowd together. This is one row. A real crystal repeats in three directions, a few thousand times in each.")
FIG['m_ew']=_panel('ewCv','Animation with controls: seen from above, a lattice of possible diffraction directions turns with the crystal, and each point diffracts as it crosses a circle set by the wavelength; beside it the detector, face on, collects every spot recorded so far',
 [_sl('eA','Size of the repeating unit','10 Å',6,14,1,10),_sl('eE','X-ray energy','12.66 keV',8,16,0.02,12.66),'<div class="btns"><button id="eR">Start again</button></div>'],
 [('Spots recorded','eN'),('The crystal has turned','eT'),('Finest detail at the detector edge','eD')],
 "Left, seen from above. The dots are the directions in which the crystal could diffract, one layer of them, laid out on a lattice that turns with the crystal. It is called the reciprocal lattice, and the space it lies in, reciprocal space. The gray circle is the Ewald sphere, seen in cross-section, named for Paul Ewald. Its radius is set by the wavelength, and it is Bragg's law drawn as a picture: a dot diffracts only at the moment it crosses the circle. When one does, an orange ray leaves the crystal and strikes the detector. Right: the detector seen face on, keeping every spot it has caught, from all the layers. A still crystal would give only the few spots that happen to lie on the circle. Turning it sweeps the rest through. A larger repeating unit packs the dots closer, and a higher energy makes the circle larger and squeezes the pattern toward the middle. The repeating unit here is about ten times smaller than a protein's, so that the dots can be told apart. A protein crystal gives the same picture with about a thousand times as many spots.")
FIG['m_fou']=_panel('fouCv','Interactive: the electron density of five atoms on a line, built up one wave at a time from the spots, with a choice of the right phases, zero phases, random phases or the phases of a different molecule',
 ['<div class="btns" style="grid-column:1/-1"><button id="fBt" aria-pressed="true">The right phases</button><button id="fBz" aria-pressed="false">All phases zero</button><button id="fBr" aria-pressed="false">Random phases</button><button id="fBo" aria-pressed="false">Phases from another molecule</button></div>',
  _slw('fN','Spots included','4 spots',0,20,1,4)],
 [('Finest wave included','fR'),('Phases used','fP'),('Match to the true density','fC')],
 "A molecule of five atoms on a line, repeated every 20 Å. Each bar is one spot: its height is the strength of the spot, and the number under it is the spacing of the wave it stands for. Slide to add the waves one at a time. The coarse waves give the rough outline, and the fine ones sharpen it until the two atoms 1.5 Å apart come apart. Then change the phases. With the strengths unchanged and the phases wrong, the atoms are lost. With the phases of a different molecule, shown by the purple rings, the peaks move to where that molecule's atoms are. The phases carry most of the picture.")
FIG['m_map']=_panel('mapCv','Interactive: the map of one helix of myoglobin, thirteen amino acids long, at a chosen resolution, drawn as a wire mesh or as a filled outline, with its model of atoms and bonds',
 [_sl('pD','Resolution of the data','2.0 Å',-6,-1,0.1,-2),_sl('pS','Contour level','40% of the highest peak',20,70,1,40),'<div class="btns"><button id="pWm" aria-pressed="true">Wire mesh</button><button id="pWo" aria-pressed="false">Outline</button></div>','<div class="btns"><button id="pB" aria-pressed="true">Show the model</button></div>'],
 [('What the map shows','pW')],
 "The map of one piece of a real protein: amino acids 4 to 16 of myoglobin, part of its first helix, with 113 atoms. A structure is built from many pieces like it. The helix is seen from the side. The blue mesh is the surface of the map at the chosen contour level, drawn the way crystallographic programs draw it: as a net of wires, so that the model can be seen through it. The curved wires are where flat sheets facing you cut the surface, and the straight ones are sheets seen edge on. The Outline button shows the same surface as a single line around a shaded area, which is easier to read when the resolution is low. Hide the model and try to place the atoms from the map alone, as a crystallographer has to. The map is made by spreading each atom into a round cloud whose width grows with the resolution, and adding the clouds up. A map calculated from real spots would also show faint ripples, from stopping the sum of waves at a sharp limit. The positions of the atoms are from entry 1MBN in the Protein Data Bank.")
FIG['m_dif']=_panel('difCv','Interactive: the helix of myoglobin with its map as a pale mesh and a model in which one amino acid is wrong. Buttons choose the mistake: a ring turned, a side chain turned, or atoms left out. A menu chooses the amino acid, and a slider or a dial on the bond sets how far it is turned, or how many atoms are built. Green mesh marks where the data call for more density than the model has, and red mesh where they call for less',
 ['<div class="btns" style="grid-column:1/-1"><button id="dfA" aria-pressed="true">A ring turned</button><button id="dfB" aria-pressed="false">A side chain turned</button><button id="dfC" aria-pressed="false">Atoms left out</button></div>',
  '<div class="ctl"><label for="dfR">Amino acid</label><select id="dfR"></select></div>',
  _sl('dfT','Turned by','180°',0,355,5,180),_sl('dfN','Atoms built','0 of 4',0,4,1,0),
  '<div class="btns"><button id="dfD">Correct it</button></div>'],
 [('Amino acid with the mistake','dfW'),('What the difference map says','dfS')],
 "The same piece of myoglobin as in the map figure, at 2 Å, with a model that is wrong in one amino acid. The pale blue mesh is the map. The green mesh is where the data call for more density than the model has, and the red mesh is where they call for less. Choose a kind of mistake and the amino acid to make it in. A ring or a side chain turns about the bond marked in orange: drag the knob on the dial around that bond, or use the slider. The mark at the top of the dial is the right position, and as the knob comes round to it the red and green shrink and vanish. Atoms left out of the model show as green alone, and the slider builds them back one at a time. Everywhere else the model is right, and there the difference map is flat: it shows the mistakes and nothing else. The difference density here is worked out directly from the true positions of the atoms. In a real structure it is calculated with phases from the model, which makes it weaker (a missing atom comes back at about half its height) and adds noise, so it is usually drawn at three times its typical value.")
FIG['m_ref']=_panel('refCv','Interactive: four atoms on a line that can be dragged or refined automatically, the difference map above them, and bars comparing each measured spot with the value calculated from the model',
 ['<div class="btns" style="grid-column:1/-1"><button id="rBa">Refine</button><button id="rBs">Shake the atoms</button><button id="rBw">Start from a wrong model</button></div>'],
 [('R','rR'),('R-free','rF'),('Error in the atom positions','rE')],
 "A structure of four atoms on a line. The measured spot strengths come from the true structure, which is hidden. Drag the atoms of the model, or press Refine, and watch three things. The dark bars move toward the pale ones as the model comes to explain the measurements. R, the overall disagreement, falls. And the difference map goes flat: green and red mark where the data want more density, or less, than the model has. R-free is the same sum over three spots that the refinement is never allowed to use. The measurements have a little noise in them, so neither number reaches zero. Then start from a wrong model, with atoms in each other's places, and press Refine. It stops at a poor answer. Refinement polishes a model that is nearly right and cannot rescue one that is not. The last read-out can be shown only because this is a test case. It allows for the whole model being slid along the line or mirrored, which the spots cannot detect.")
FIG['m_dose']=_panel('doseCv','Interactive: a diffraction pattern whose outer spots fade as the absorbed dose rises, beside a curve of the brightness left against the detail the spots carry',
 [_slw('dD','Dose absorbed by the crystal','0 MGy',0,90,1,0)],
 [('Spots below half brightness','dH'),('Brightness left at 2 Å','d2'),('Against the 30 MGy limit','dL')],
 "Left: a diffraction pattern, with the fine detail toward the edge. Right: how much of the original brightness is left at each level of detail. As the dose builds up the spots fade from the outside in. The rule of thumb drawn here is that spots lose half their brightness after about 10 MGy for each ångström of the detail they carry, so the 2 Å spots are down to half at 20 MGy. Real crystals vary around this. Damage to particular groups, such as the breaking of disulfide bonds, starts at much lower doses.")

MXG=dict(key='mx',title='How MX Works',name='How MX works',kicker='NE-CAT guides · macromolecular crystallography',h1='How MX works',
  lede="Macromolecular crystallography, MX for short, finds where the atoms are in the large molecules of life: proteins, DNA and RNA, and machines as large as a ribosome or the shell of a virus. It does this by shining X-rays on a crystal of the molecule and working back from the pattern of spots the crystal throws onto a detector. This guide explains why the shape of a molecule is worth knowing, why the method needs a crystal, what the spots are, and how they are turned into a structure that can be trusted.",
  hero='m_hero',table='none',bar='',
  sections=[
   dict(id='why',nav='Why structures',h2='Big molecules, and why their shapes matter',prose=[
     "Macromolecules are the large molecules of life: proteins, DNA and RNA, and the assemblies they build together. They are large by the standards of chemistry. Myoglobin, a small protein, has about 1,260 atoms, not counting hydrogen. A ribosome has about 150,000, and the shell of a virus several hundred thousand. Crystallography can find the positions of nearly all of them. Hydrogen atoms are usually too faint to see, and parts of a molecule that do not hold still in the crystal do not show up.",
     "A protein is a chain of amino acids, folded into a particular shape, as the short guide {guide:prot} shows. The chain can be short or long: insulin has 51 amino acids, a typical protein has a few hundred, and the largest have thousands. The shape is a large part of what lets a protein do its job, but not the whole of it. The chemistry of the amino acids matters as well, and so does the way the protein moves. An enzyme has a pocket that fits one molecule and no other, lined with the chemical groups that carry out its reaction. An antibody has a surface that matches its target in shape and in charge. Many proteins change shape as they work. A structure shows all of this at once: which atoms are where, what they are next to, and, from structures caught in different states, how they move.",
     "Knowing where the atoms are turns a name in a genome into a mechanism. It shows how an enzyme speeds up its reaction and why a single changed amino acid causes a disease. It shows where a drug binds and what could be changed to make it bind better. Many of the medicines in use today were designed with the structure of their target in hand.",
     "The Protein Data Bank, the public archive of these structures, passed 250,000 entries in March 2026. Most of them were determined by X-ray crystallography."],
    items=[('fig','m_gal')]),
   dict(id='xrays',nav='Why X-rays',h2='Why X-rays, and why there is no X-ray microscope',prose=[
     "Large as these molecules are, their atoms are no bigger than any others. Bonded atoms are about 1.5 Å apart: fifteen billionths of a centimeter. Light cannot show detail much finer than half its wavelength, and the wavelength of visible light is several thousand ångströms. Seeing atoms takes light with a wavelength of about one ångström, and that is an X-ray.",
     "There is a second difficulty. A microscope uses a lens to gather the light scattered by an object and bring it back together as an image. No lens can do that for X-rays finely enough to show atoms. In crystallography the scattered X-rays are recorded as they are, and the work of the lens is done afterward by calculation."],
    items=[('fig','m_scale'),('eqs',['m_res'])]),
   dict(id='crystal',nav='Why a crystal',h2='Why a crystal: one molecule is far too faint',prose=[
     "X-rays are scattered by electrons, and a single protein molecule does not have many. The scatter from one molecule is far too weak to measure, and the X-rays needed to try would destroy it first.",
     "A crystal solves both problems. It is the same molecule repeated in the same orientation: about a hundred billion times, for a typical crystal. The repeat runs in all three dimensions: rows side by side make a layer, and layers stack to make the crystal. Each copy scatters the same faint wave. In most directions those waves are out of step and cancel. In certain directions they are exactly in step, and add. The scatter of the whole crystal is gathered into those directions as sharp, bright spots.",
     "The crystal also shares out the damage. Every X-ray that is absorbed damages a molecule somewhere, but the pattern is the average over all the copies, and it survives until a good fraction of them have been hit.",
     "The price is that the molecule has to be persuaded to crystallize, which is often the hardest step of all. The short guide {guide:grow} shows how it is done. A protein crystal is a fragile thing, about half water, held together by a few weak contacts between neighbors. That water is also why the structure in a crystal is close to the one the molecule has in the cell."],
    items=[('fig','m_lat'),('eqs',['m_lat'])]),
   dict(id='pattern',nav='The pattern',h2='Where the spots fall, and why the crystal is turned',prose=[
     "The positions of the spots say nothing about the molecule. They are set by the lattice: the size and shape of the unit cell, the block that repeats to make the crystal. A large unit cell gives spots that are close together, and a small one gives spots that are far apart.",
     "Each spot can be thought of as a reflection of the beam from one set of parallel planes through the lattice. A set of planes reflects only when the beam meets it at exactly the right angle: the angle at which the waves reflected from one plane and the next are exactly in step. That condition is Bragg's law, named for William Lawrence Bragg, who worked it out in 1912. It ties the angle of a spot to the spacing of its planes and the wavelength of the X-rays, and it is given with the equations below. With the crystal held still, very few sets of planes are at their angle, and only a few spots appear.",
     "So the crystal is turned in the beam while the detector records. The figure below shows why, using a construction called the Ewald sphere. As it turns, one set of planes after another comes to its angle, flashes, and passes on. A full data set is a few hundred to a few thousand images covering a half turn or more, and can hold hundreds of thousands of spots.",
     "How far the crystal must turn depends on its symmetry. The unit cell often holds several copies of the molecule, each a turned or shifted copy of the others. Proteins are built from amino acids of one handedness, so their crystals cannot contain mirror images, and that leaves 65 ways of arranging the copies, called space groups. Symmetry in the crystal makes sets of spots equal in brightness, so a crystal of high symmetry gives a complete data set from a smaller turn.",
     "The angle of a spot from the direct beam says how fine a detail it reports on. Spots near the middle of the detector come from widely spaced planes and carry the overall shape of the molecule. Spots near the edge come from closely spaced planes and carry the fine detail. The resolution of a data set is the spacing of the finest planes that still give measurable spots. The guide {guide:overview} shows how the beam energy and the detector distance set what the detector can catch, and {guide:detintro} shows how the detector counts the X-rays in each spot."],
    items=[('fig','m_ew'),('eqs',['m_bragg','m_spacing'])]),
   dict(id='waves',nav='Spots as waves',h2='What a spot is: one wave of electron density',prose=[
     "The positions of the spots come from the lattice. Their brightness comes from the molecule, and this is where the structure is.",
     "What X-rays see is electron density: how many electrons there are at each point in the unit cell. Picture it as a landscape, with a peak at every atom. Because the crystal repeats, the landscape repeats, and a repeating pattern has a property that Joseph Fourier found in the early 1800s. It can be built exactly by adding up simple waves, and the only waves needed are those that fit the repeat a whole number of times: one crest across the unit cell, then two, then three, and so on. A sum of this kind is called a Fourier series. Its broad waves give the general shape of the landscape, and its finer and finer waves give the detail.",
     "Each spot in the diffraction pattern is one wave of that series. The position of the spot says which wave it is: the direction its crests run in, and how many of them cross the unit cell. Those counts are the three index numbers of the spot, written h, k and l. The brightness of the spot says how tall the wave is: the height is the square root of the brightness. In effect, the crystal takes its own electron density apart into waves and sends each one to a different place on the detector.",
     "To add a wave to the sum, one more thing is needed: where its crests lie within the unit cell. That is the wave's phase. Shift a wave by half its spacing and it puts a valley where there was a peak. The height and the phase of a wave together are called the structure factor of its spot.",
     "Add up all the waves, each with its height and its phase, and the electron density appears. This sum is called a Fourier synthesis, and it is the calculation that does the work of the missing lens. Leave out the fine waves, and the result is a blurred version of the density. That is what resolution means: a 3 Å data set has no waves finer than 3 Å in it."],
    items=[('fig','m_fou'),('eqs',['m_sf','m_synth'])]),
   dict(id='phase',nav='The phase problem',h2='The catch: the phases are not recorded',prose=[
     "The detector records how bright each spot is. That gives the height of each wave. It does not record the phase, and as the figure above shows, the phases matter more than the heights. Half of what is needed to build the density is missing.",
     "This is the phase problem, and every structure determination has to get around it. There are several ways to do so, and each is a subject of its own. Later guides will take them up. For the rest of this guide, take it that the phases have been found."],
    items=[('eqs',['m_int'])]),
   dict(id='map',nav='Map and model',h2='The map, and the model built into it',prose=[
     "With heights and phases for all the spots, the waves can be added up. The result is the electron density map: a three-dimensional picture of where the electrons are in the unit cell. It is drawn as a surface, like a net, around the regions where the density is high.",
     "The map is not yet a structure. A structure is a list of atoms and their positions, and someone, or some program, has to work out which atoms account for the density. The amino acid sequence is known, so the task is to thread a known chain through the map.",
     "A macromolecule has thousands of atoms to place, so the figure below looks at one short piece: thirteen amino acids of a helix of myoglobin. How hard the job is depends on the resolution. Near 1 Å, individual atoms can be seen. By 1.5 Å they have begun to merge, though rings still show the holes in their middles. At 2 Å the shapes of the side chains are clear, and ordered water molecules show up. At 3 Å the chain can be traced and the larger side chains placed. At 4 Å helices appear as rods, and the model rests heavily on what is already known about how proteins are built."],
    items=[('fig','m_map')]),
   dict(id='refine',nav='Refinement',h2='Refinement: making the model fit, and knowing when to stop',prose=[
     "A first model is roughly right. Refinement makes it better. From the model, the brightness of every spot can be calculated and compared with what was measured. The atoms are then moved, a little at a time, to make the two agree, while bond lengths and angles are kept close to their known values.",
     "Each round improves the phases too, because the phases now come from a better model. The new map shows things the old one did not: a side chain in a different position, a bound water, a ligand. The model is corrected, and the cycle repeats.",
     "A special map guides the corrections. The difference map shows where the data call for more density than the model has, and where they call for less. In the first figure below it is drawn in green and red over a model with a mistake in it. An atom in the wrong place sits in red, where the data call for less, and leaves green where it belongs. The second figure shows the same thing for four atoms on a line, and lets the model be refined.",
     "The danger is fitting the model to the noise. The check is to set aside a few percent of the spots at the very start and never use them in refinement. If the model is truly improving, it will predict those spots better too. That score is called R-free, and it is the first number to look at in judging a structure, along with the resolution.",
     "Refinement stops when further changes no longer lower R-free and the difference map is flat. The model is then checked against what is known of chemistry, apart from the data: its bond lengths and angles, the twists of its backbone, and whether any atoms sit too close together. Last, the structure is deposited in the Protein Data Bank together with the measured spots, so that anyone can recalculate the map and judge the model for themselves."],
    items=[('fig','m_dif'),('fig','m_ref'),('eqs',['m_R','m_obs'])]),
   dict(id='damage',nav='Limits',h2='What limits a structure',prose=[
     "<b>The crystal.</b> No measurement can show more than the crystal holds. If the molecules are not all in quite the same position and orientation, the fine waves blur out, and the spots at the edge of the detector fade into the background. Much of the work of crystallography is in finding conditions that give better-ordered crystals.",
     "<b>Radiation damage.</b> The X-rays that give the pattern also destroy the crystal. Each absorbed photon sets loose electrons that break bonds. Crystals are held in a stream of nitrogen gas at 100 K, which slows the spread of the damage many times over, but does not stop it. The fine detail goes first. The guide {guide:cold} explains why the cold helps.",
     "<b>Many crystals.</b> When no one crystal can give a full data set, the data are gathered from many: a few images from each of hundreds or thousands of small crystals, combined afterward. This is serial crystallography. It spreads the dose so thinly that data can be collected at room temperature, where the molecule moves as it does in life, and with short enough exposures it can follow a reaction as it happens.",
     "<b>The beam.</b> A crystal can take only so much dose, however it is delivered. What a brighter and smaller beam offers is the chance to use smaller crystals, to pick out the best part of a crystal, and to collect quickly. The guides {guide:aps} and {guide:overview} describe where that beam comes from.",
     "Crystallography is not the only route to a structure. Nuclear magnetic resonance, NMR, gives the structures of small proteins in solution, with no crystal at all. Electron microscopy of frozen single particles needs no crystal and does well with large assemblies. Computer prediction gives a good first model for many proteins. Crystallography remains the most direct route to fine detail, and to seeing exactly how a drug, a substrate or a water molecule sits in its site. It is also fast: once crystals are in hand, a data set takes minutes or less."],
    items=[('fig','m_dose'),('eqs',['m_dose'])]),
  ],
  further="""        <li>B. Rupp, <i>Biomolecular Crystallography: Principles, Practice, and Application to Structural Biology</i> (2010). The standard full-length textbook.</li>
        <li>G. Rhodes, <i>Crystallography Made Crystal Clear</i>, 3rd edition (2006). A shorter introduction for readers of structures.</li>
        <li>J. Drenth, <i>Principles of Protein X-ray Crystallography</i>, 3rd edition (2007). The physics of diffraction and phasing.</li>
        <li>A. T. Brünger, "Free R value: a novel statistical quantity for assessing the accuracy of crystal structures", <i>Nature</i> 355, 472 (1992). The origin of R-free.</li>
        <li>R. L. Owen, E. Rudiño-Piñera and E. F. Garman, "Experimental determination of the radiation dose limit for cryocooled protein crystals", <i>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA</i> 103, 4912 (2006). The 30 MGy limit.</li>
        <li>M. R. Howells et al., "An assessment of the resolution limitation due to radiation-damage in X-ray diffraction microscopy", <i>J. Electron Spectrosc. Relat. Phenom.</i> 170, 4 (2009). The rule of 10 MGy for each ångström.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.rcsb.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RCSB Protein Data Bank</a>. The archive of structures, with teaching material at PDB-101.</li>""")
